2. Highest Impact Improvements

These give the largest speedup WITHOUT weakening the model.

A. Use Mixed Precision (MANDATORY)

Current:

FP32 training

Should become:

AMP FP16 training

Add:

scaler = torch.cuda.amp.GradScaler()

Training:

with torch.cuda.amp.autocast():
    ac_out, geo_out = model(images)
    loss = ...

Backward:

scaler.scale(loss).backward()
scaler.step(optimizer)
scaler.update()

Expected:

1.5–2.5× faster
lower VRAM
larger batch possible

This is the single safest optimization.

B. Move Spectrogram Pipeline OFF CPU

Currently ALL this happens in Dataset workers:

mel_spec = self.mel_transform(waveform)
log_mel = torch.log(...)
F.interpolate(...)
normalize(...)

This is very expensive.

Better Architecture

Dataset should return ONLY waveform + targets.

Move ALL spectrogram processing to GPU training loop.

Dataset:

return waveform, coords, target

Then inside training loop:

waveforms = waveforms.to(device)

with autocast():
    mel = mel_transform(waveforms)

Then:

resize
normalization
masking

ALL on GPU.

This usually gives massive throughput improvement.

C. Use Persistent Workers

Current:

DataLoader(... num_workers=4)

Use:

DataLoader(
    ...,
    num_workers=4,
    pin_memory=True,
    persistent_workers=True,
    prefetch_factor=2
)

This avoids worker respawn every epoch.

Important for Kaggle.

D. Enable TF32

On T4/A100/L4:

torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_tf32 = True
torch.backends.cudnn.allow_tf32 = True

No meaningful accuracy loss.

Often:

10–30% speedup

Especially ViTs.

E. Compile the Model

PyTorch 2.x:

model = torch.compile(model)

Especially good for:

ViTs
repeated static shapes

Typical:

10–25% faster
F. Increase Batch Size

You use:

BATCH_SIZE = 16

Very likely underutilizing GPU.

After AMP:
try:

32
48
64

until VRAM saturation.

ViTs scale very well with larger batches.

G. Use Channels Last

After model creation:

model = model.to(memory_format=torch.channels_last)

Input tensors:

images = images.to(memory_format=torch.channels_last)

Good for:

Conv layers
ViT patch embedding
3. Spectrogram-Level Optimizations

These matter enormously.

A. Precompute Mel Spectrograms (OPTIONAL BUT HUGE)

Currently:

every epoch recomputes FFTs

This is expensive.

You can precompute:

waveform → mel → save .npy

Then training loads mel tensors directly.

Benefits:

huge epoch speedup
deterministic preprocessing

Tradeoff:

storage size increases

This is one of the most common BirdCLEF optimizations.

B. Cache Soundscape Mel Files

Soundscapes are long and reused many times.

Instead of:

decoding full audio repeatedly

Do:

preprocess once
save mel tensors

Huge gain.

C. Use Full-Audio Spectrogram Slicing

Instead of:

FFT per 5s crop

Do:

one full spectrogram
slice spectrogram windows

This avoids redundant FFT computation.

Especially important for:

overlapping windows
soundscapes
4. Dataloader Optimizations
A. Avoid Resampler Creation Per Sample

Current:

resampler = T.Resample(sample_rate, CFG.SR)

inside _load_audio.

Very expensive.

Instead:

create global resampler cache

Example:

RESAMPLERS = {}

Then reuse.

B. Avoid Repeated Tensor Creation

Current:

torch.tensor(data)

many times.

Use:

torch.from_numpy(data)

Zero-copy.

Faster.

C. Avoid Min-Max Normalization Per Sample

This:

(log_mel - min)/(max-min)

is expensive and unstable.

Instead use:

fixed normalization
dataset-level mean/std

This improves:

stability
speed
consistency
D. Use Non-Blocking GPU Transfer
images = images.to(device, non_blocking=True)

Requires:

pin_memory=True

in DataLoader.

5. ViT-Specific Optimizations
A. Use Smaller Patch Models

Current:

vit_base_patch16_224

Alternatives:

Model	Speed	Accuracy
ViT-Base	slow	strong
ViT-Small	faster	slightly lower
ConvNeXt-Tiny	much faster	often competitive
EfficientNet-B2	very fast	good

Best compromise often:

ConvNeXt

for BirdCLEF.

B. Freeze Early Layers Initially

Epochs 1–2:

for param in backbone.blocks[:6]:
    param.requires_grad = False

Benefits:

faster backward
stabilizes training

Then unfreeze later.

No major accuracy loss usually.

C. Gradient Checkpointing

If VRAM limited:

model.set_grad_checkpointing(True)

Trade:

slower compute
larger batch possible

Useful only if memory bottlenecked.

6. Geo-KNN Optimization

Your current KNN is elegant but computationally expensive.

A. Replace sklearn with FAISS

Use:

FAISS

Especially for:

large embedding banks
many validation windows

Massive speedup possible.

B. Only Run KNN on Uncertain Predictions

Current:

KNN runs for ALL samples.

Instead:

uncertain_mask = max_prob < threshold

Only run KNN there.

Huge validation speedup.

No quality loss.

7. Validation Optimizations

Validation is currently expensive.

A. Vectorize KNN Fusion

Current:

for i in range(len(probs_visual)):

Bad.

Instead:

mask = np.max(probs_visual, axis=1) <= threshold

final_probs = probs_visual.copy()

final_probs[mask] = (
    probs_visual[mask] * (1 - alpha)
    + probs_knn[mask] * alpha
)

Much faster.

B. Avoid Repeated CPU Transfers

Current:

.cpu().numpy()

many times.

Keep tensors on GPU longer.

Transfer once.

8. Training Strategy Optimizations
A. Cosine Scheduler

You currently use fixed LR.

Use:

CosineAnnealingLR

or:

OneCycleLR

Improves convergence efficiency.

B. Gradient Accumulation

If VRAM limited:

effective_batch = batch_size * accumulation_steps

Maintains optimization quality.

C. EMA Weights

Use:

Exponential Moving Average

Improves:

generalization
validation stability

without slowing inference.

